 The `async` keyword in C# is used to declare methods, lambda expressions, and anonymous methods as asynchronous. Asynchronous methods allow your application to perform long-running or I/O-bound tasks without blocking the UI or other tasks. This improves the overall performance and responsiveness of your application.

When you mark a method as asynchronous with the `async` keyword, you must also use the `await` keyword to specify points where the method will yield control back to the calling thread, allowing other tasks to run. This is done to ensure that the asynchronous method doesn't block the calling thread, which would defeat the purpose of asynchronous programming.

Here's an example of an asynchronous method in C#:

```csharp
public async Task<string> GetWebContentAsync(string url)
{
    using (HttpClient client = new HttpClient())
    {
        string content = await client.GetStringAsync(url);
        return content;
    }
}
```

In this example, the `GetWebContentAsync` method is asynchronous (declared with the `async` keyword), and it uses the `await` keyword to wait for the `GetStringAsync` method to complete before returning the content. This ensures that the calling thread is not blocked while waiting for the web content to be retrieved.

